---
intent: >
  Extend aide_info from a staleness-only surface into a dual-concern boot
  reporter that returns both `outdated[]` (unchanged behavior) and a new
  `brain` tagged union describing whether the host's obsidian MCP entry is
  configured and whether its vault directory exists on disk.
---

## Project Structure

After this plan executes, `src/tools/info/` contains the existing orchestrator
alongside a new `buildBrainState` helper. The orchestrator composes the existing
staleness logic with the new brain-state helper and returns both as independent
top-level fields.

```
src/tools/info/
├── .aide                             ← [existing] intent spec for this orchestrator
├── plan.aide                         ← [this file] implementation plan
├── index.ts                          ← [modified] orchestrator: info(root: string) => Promise<InfoResult>
├── index.test.ts                     ← [modified] co-located tests covering staleness + brain branches
└── buildBrainState/                  ← [new] helper: resolve host's brain precondition state
    ├── index.ts                      ← [new] buildBrainState(root: string) => Promise<BrainState>
    └── index.test.ts                 ← [new] co-located unit tests for the helper's five branches
```

Types live in the central `src/types/index.ts` — no type files inside this
module. The module-scoped tree ends here; `src/types/index.ts` is outside this
plan's scope directory and is edited in step 1 as an explicit cross-cutting
change.

## Plan

### 1. Update shared types in `src/types/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] 1a. Add a new exported type `BrainState` as a tagged union keyed on
      `status`. Shape: `{ status: 'ok' | 'no-mcp-entry' | 'invalid-path',
      vaultPath: string | null }`. Use a string-literal union for `status`
      (per the type-system playbook's "string literal unions" rule — no enum).
      `vaultPath` is `string | null`: non-null for `ok` (the resolved path)
      and for `invalid-path` (the configured-but-unresolvable path); `null`
      for `no-mcp-entry`.
- [x] 1b. Extend the existing `InfoResult` interface with a new required
      field `brain: BrainState`. Keep `serverVersion: string` and
      `outdated: string[]` unchanged. Update the interface's JSDoc to
      describe both concerns (staleness + brain precondition) as independent
      top-level fields.
- [x] 1c. Export both `BrainState` and `InfoResult` from the same file; no
      new types file is created (all shared types live in `src/types/` per
      folder-structure's "Types Organization" rule).

### 2. Create `buildBrainState/` helper

Read: `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`, `coding-playbook/services/error-handling`

- [x] 2a. Create `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/index.ts` with a default
      export: `async function buildBrainState(root: string): Promise<BrainState>`.
      Single `root` argument (the host project root) — matches the existing
      `info(root)` signature and the "single input object" rule's intent
      (one argument is fine; the rule kicks in at two+).
- [x] 2b. Reuse `detectFramework` from
      `@/tools/init/detectFramework/index.js` to resolve the host's framework
      config. Pass only `root` (no framework override) so detection defaults
      to Claude Code for cold projects — this is the inherited "cold project"
      semantic the spec's strategy section codifies.
- [x] 2c. Resolve the MCP config path by joining `root` with
      `frameworkConfig.mcpConfigPath` (the `FRAMEWORK_CONFIGS` map's
      `.mcp.json` / `.cursor/mcp.json` / etc. per framework — do NOT
      duplicate that mapping here).
- [x] 2d. Implement the pipeline declaratively (each step is an early
      return per the conventions note's "Early Returns Over Nesting" rule;
      no nested try/catch):
      1. Read the MCP config file. Missing file (ENOENT) or any read
         failure → return `{ status: 'no-mcp-entry', vaultPath: null }`.
      2. Parse JSON. Parse failure → return `{ status: 'no-mcp-entry',
         vaultPath: null }`. (Mirrors `wireMcp`'s malformed-JSON handling:
         map to a structured state, never rethrow.)
      3. Extract `config.mcpServers ?? {}`. Dual-key existence check: if
         `"obsidian"` key is NOT in `servers`, return `{ status:
         'no-mcp-entry', vaultPath: null }`. The spec's strategy explicitly
         mirrors `provisionBrain.buildObsidianMcpStep`'s single-key check
         on `"obsidian"`. There is no documented fallback key for obsidian
         — the dual-key pattern exists for the aide entry (`"aide"` /
         `"aidemd-mcp"`) in `wireMcp`, not for obsidian in `provisionBrain`.
         Keep this check single-key to match `provisionBrain` exactly, and
         document the alignment in a source comment.
      4. Extract the entry's vault path. Read `servers.obsidian.args` and
         take the LAST positional element (matches
         `provisionBrain.obsidianMcpEntry`'s shape:
         `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", <vaultPath>]` on POSIX, or `["/c", "npx",
         "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", <vaultPath>]` on Windows — the vault path
         is always the final positional arg). If `args` is missing, not an
         array, empty, or the last element is not a non-empty string →
         return `{ status: 'no-mcp-entry', vaultPath: null }` (treats
         "configured-but-unparseable" as equivalent to "not configured" per
         the spec's strategy note).
      5. Stat the candidate vault path with `fs/promises.access` (or
         `stat`). Resolves to an existing directory → return
         `{ status: 'ok', vaultPath: <candidatePath> }`. Fails or the
         target is not a directory → return `{ status: 'invalid-path',
         vaultPath: <candidatePath> }` (the configured path is carried
         forward so the orchestrator can name the failing path in its
         message).
- [x] 2e. Write a multi-line JSDoc block on the default export explaining
      the five branches and why each collapses the way it does — this is
      the orchestrator's "decisions not descriptions" contract. Each inline
      step above gets a short `// …` comment in the body narrating its
      branch (inline narration belongs on orchestrators; the helper-as-
      orchestrator pattern applies here because `buildBrainState` itself
      orchestrates a small pipeline).
- [x] 2f. The helper must never throw. All failure modes (missing file,
      malformed JSON, missing key, bad args shape, failed stat) map to
      structured `BrainState` values — per the spec's "never thrown errors"
      desired outcome and the error-handling playbook's "structured states
      over exceptions at leaf I/O" pattern.

