## Decisions

**Tagged union over boolean for `BrainState`.** The spec explicitly forbids
collapsing `no-mcp-entry` and `invalid-path` into a single boolean because
the two states have different remediations: the former needs `/aide:init`
to run the vault-path interview from scratch, while the latter needs the
user to either restore the moved folder or update the existing entry's path.
A boolean erases that distinction; the three-state union preserves it. This
traces directly to the spec's undesired outcome forbidding the conflation.

**Three failure modes collapse to `no-mcp-entry`.** Missing MCP config
file, malformed JSON, and a valid config without the `"obsidian"` key
all resolve to the same structured state because the user's next action
is identical across them (run `/aide:init`). Separating them would create
orchestrator branches with no behavioral difference. The helper therefore
maps all three to `{ status: 'no-mcp-entry', vaultPath: null }` and never
throws.

**`invalid-path` stays distinct from `no-mcp-entry`.** When an obsidian
entry exists but the configured vault directory does not resolve, the
remediation is different: the user fixes the path in the existing entry,
not a fresh init. Keeping this branch separate also lets the
orchestrator surface the failing path to the user (which
`no-mcp-entry` cannot — no path was configured in that case). The
`vaultPath` field is populated for `invalid-path` precisely so the
orchestrator's message can name the path that failed.

**Configured-but-unparseable entry → `no-mcp-entry`, not `invalid-path`.**
An obsidian entry whose `args` array is missing, empty, or malformed is
treated as if the entry is not present. The spec's strategy section
codifies this: `invalid-path` is reserved for a correctly-shaped entry
whose stored path fails to resolve. An entry so broken we cannot extract
a path at all is closer to "entry not configured" than "path broken".
The user's remediation remains a full `/aide:init` run in that case.

**Reuse `detectFramework` rather than duplicate `FRAMEWORK_CONFIGS`.**
The init tree's `FRAMEWORK_CONFIGS` map is the single source of truth for
per-framework MCP config paths. Duplicating the mapping inside the info
tool creates a second source of truth that will silently drift when a
new framework is added. Calling `detectFramework(root)` gives the info
helper the same `mcpConfigPath` that `wireMcp` and `provisionBrain` use
to write — so detection and provisioning agree by construction.

**Single-key obsidian existence check (not dual-key).** `wireMcp` does a
dual-key check (`"aide" in servers || "aidemd-mcp" in servers`) because
there was a historical rename of the aide entry. `provisionBrain`'s
obsidian check is single-key on `"obsidian"` — no historical rename.
The info helper mirrors `provisionBrain`'s single-key check exactly so
"present" means the same thing on both sides (detection on the info side,
provisioning on the init side). A source comment in `buildBrainState`
points at this alignment so a future maintainer adding a second obsidian
key knows to update both sides at once.

**Last-positional-arg rule for vault path extraction.** The obsidian
entry constructed by `provisionBrain` always stores the vault path as
the final positional argument (`[@bitbonsai/mcpvault, <path>]` on POSIX;
`[/c, npx, @bitbonsai/mcpvault, <path>]` on Windows). Taking the last
element of `args` is platform-agnostic — it works for both shapes without
the info helper having to know the platform. The Windows-shape regression
test in step 3f pins this invariant.

**Staleness and brain state as two independent top-level fields.** The
spec's strategy section is explicit: do not fuse them into a single
status enum or collapse brain state into the `outdated` array. Each
field carries a different severity (soft notification vs. hard gate)
and the orchestrator applies a different policy to each. Returning them
independently keeps the orchestrator's boot-branch logic narrow — it
reads only the field it cares about on each branch.

**`BrainState` lives in `src/types/index.ts`, not next to the helper.**
The folder-structure playbook's "Types Organization" rule centralizes
all shared types in `src/types/`. `BrainState` is exported as part of
`InfoResult` and therefore shared across tool boundaries — it belongs
in the central types file, not in a per-module types file.

**Helper is a subfolder, not an inline function in the orchestrator.**
Progressive disclosure's aggressive-modularization rule: every helper
gets its own subfolder with `index.ts`, named after its default export.
The `buildBrainState` pipeline has five decision branches and four
failure modes; it is absolutely not a single-helper exception candidate.
Keeping it in its own folder makes it independently testable (step 3)
and keeps the orchestrator at a size where its inline pipeline
narration is the full story.

**Downstream orchestrator prompt update is out of scope but noted.**
The `/aide` orchestrator at
`.claude/commands/aide/aide.md` currently branches on `outdated` only;
once this plan ships, it must also branch on `brain`: `status === 'ok'`
continues boot, any other status hard-stops the pipeline and directs the
user to `/aide:init`. That edit lives in a different module
(`.claude/commands/aide/`) and is NOT in this plan's scope. The
top-level orchestrator running this plan must schedule a follow-up to
produce a separate `plan.aide` for that module after this plan is built.
It is not a prerequisite (this plan does not depend on it existing);
it is a dependent (that module will consume this plan's new response
shape).
